.TH ABORT 3
.SH NAME
abort, raise \- terminate abnormally, or send a signal to oneself
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B void abort(void);
.PP
.B #include <signal.h>
.PP
.B int raise(int sig);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Abort
sends SIGABRT to the calling process.
This causes a signal that normally terminates
the process with a core dump, which may be used for debugging.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page said "executes the PDP11 IOT instruction".  There is no such
instruction here and none is needed \-
.I abort
is
.B kill(getpid(), SIGABRT)
and always was on machines other than the PDP-11.  Not even the NAME of that
instruction is left: signal 6 is SIGABRT here and v7's SIGIOT is not defined
.RI ( signal (2)).
SIGABRT is left at SIG_DFL, so the kernel dumps core for it.
.PP
.B Note:
.I raise
is C11 and had no v7 ancestor.  It sends
.I sig
to the calling process and does not return until the signal has been handled.
.B There is nothing to arrange for that here:
a
.IR kill (2)
to one's own process raises the signal in the kernel's pending set, and the
system call's own return path delivers it \- the handler has run and returned
through the signal frame by the time
.I raise
yields a value.  b6sim delivers at the same point.
.PP
.IR Raise 's
value is
.IR kill 's,
0 or \-1, rather than C11's "nonzero if unsuccessful" \- the same thing said two
ways.
.SH SEE ALSO
signal(2), exit(3), kill(2), assert(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Usually `IOT trap \- core dumped' from the shell.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/abort.c, raise.c
.SH BUGS
.B There is no test for abort, deliberately.
Under b6sim a
.IR kill (2)
the guest does not catch is serviced by the simulator killing
.I its own
process \- the guest pid is the host pid \- so a test would take the simulator
down with the program and report as a harness crash rather than a result.  Under
the real kernel it is an ordinary signal and dumps core.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.I Raise
is the
.I only
routine libc gained when signal delivery landed, and the absences around it are
the point.
.PP
The kernel builds the signal frame on the user stack and
.B plants the return path in it:
a
.B "$77 SYS_sigret"
word, copied out above the frame and named as the handler's return address, so a
handler's ordinary return trips an extracode and the kernel reloads the frame.
.PP
So there is no trampoline of the kind other ports carry, no libc table shadowing
the kernel's dispositions, and
.IR signal (2)
still answers with the
.I true
previous disposition \- including the SIG_DFL a caught signal is reset to on
delivery.
.PP
It could not have been libc's work in any case: which
.I half
of a word to resume at lives in the saved program status, and only the
privileged return instruction restores it, so a handler interrupted from a
right-half instruction can be resumed only by the kernel.
